macho_trie_exports_arm64, macho_trie_exports_info_arm64 -- arm64 Mach-O dylibs
whose symbol table was stripped and whose export trie still names everything.

What they are
  Built from macho_trie_exports.c, the source of GH #1349:
    _audit_exported_function @ 0x328 (__TEXT,__text)
    _audit_caller            @ 0x340 (__TEXT,__text), calls the function above
    _audit_exported_data     @ 0x4000 (__DATA,__data)
  `llvm-strip --strip-all` removes every nlist entry (LC_SYMTAB nsyms 0, the
  LC_DYSYMTAB ranges empty) and keeps the export trie, the layout Apple's
  `strip -N` produces.
  macho_trie_exports_arm64 (33,344 bytes, SHA-256
  b9a8ae16c3f999b8243b7cb25ea3662d29eb443e70f7ff78ba68120e1fef988a) carries
  LC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS, LC_DYLD_EXPORTS_TRIE and LC_FUNCTION_STARTS.
  macho_trie_exports_info_arm64 (33,280 bytes, SHA-256
  5df31997aa4baa5a9f2c03dc6d42e6fc5ed5bd96b0fe4cde71809bd0cf4b8daa) is the
  classic layout: the trie is the LC_DYLD_INFO_ONLY export range, and it has no
  LC_FUNCTION_STARTS, so the trie is the only record of where its functions are.

How they were built (no macOS SDK; host clang + the rustup-bundled ld64.lld,
llvm-strip from the mstorsjo/llvm-mingw image)
  clang -target arm64-apple-macos11 -O0 -g0 -c macho_trie_exports.c -o t.o
  LLD=$(rustc --print sysroot)/lib/rustlib/$(rustc -vV | sed -n 's/host: //p')/bin/gcc-ld/ld64.lld
  "$LLD" -arch arm64 -dylib -platform_version macos 11.0 11.0 -fixup_chains \
         -install_name @rpath/libtrie.dylib -o chained.dylib t.o
  "$LLD" -arch arm64 -dylib -platform_version macos 11.0 11.0 -no_fixup_chains \
         -no_function_starts -install_name @rpath/libtrie.dylib -o info.dylib t.o
  docker run --rm -v $PWD:/w -w /w mstorsjo/llvm-mingw:latest sh -c \
    'llvm-strip --strip-all chained.dylib -o macho_trie_exports_arm64 &&
     llvm-strip --strip-all info.dylib -o macho_trie_exports_info_arm64'
  Ubuntu clang 14.0.0-1ubuntu1.1, LLD 20.1.8 (rustc 1.90.0), llvm-strip 23.1.4.
  Prebuilt because CI has no Mach-O linker.

What they pin
  kuna functions lists _audit_exported_function and _audit_caller (base: sub_328
  and sub_340; the classic one: no functions at all); decompile by either
  spelling of either name succeeds, and _audit_caller reads
  `_audit_exported_function(a0) + _audit_exported_data`, as the unstripped
  dylib does. audit_exported_data answers that it names data at 0x4000.
